/**
\page native_network Native C++ network API

Include `pdg/net/network.h` and link the optional `pdg-net` target in a native
C++20 application. Configure the root project with `-DPDG_BUILD_NATIVE_NETWORK=ON`.
The option defaults to OFF. This library is independent of script bindings and
is excluded from Node packages and Web builds. It reuses PDG's pinned native
WebTransport dependencies; see `src/sys/webtransport/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt`.

\section native_network_usage Runtime and callbacks

Construct a pdg::NetRuntime on your application thread and call poll() regularly
from that thread. DNS runs on a resolver thread; socket I/O and callbacks are
nonblocking and dispatched by poll(). All API methods, object destruction, and
callbacks belong to the runtime thread. Recursive poll() calls throw
`std::logic_error`. Destroy the runtime last; its destructor cancels operations,
closes sockets, suppresses callbacks, and joins its resolver thread.

Connections belong to the runtime until close, even if an application drops its
last public shared pointer. Destroying a client cancels its pending connect;
established connections remain usable. Destroying a server closes its sessions.
A server retains accepted connections. Calling shutdown(false) stops admission
and retains established sessions until explicitly closed or the server is
destroyed. close(false) drains queued reliable output; close(true) discards it.
Repeated close calls produce at most one close callback.

\code{.cpp}
#include "pdg/net/network.h"

pdg::NetRuntime runtime;
pdg::NetClient client(runtime);
pdg::NetConnectionPtr connection;
pdg::NetConnectOptions endpoint;
endpoint.host = "127.0.0.1";
endpoint.port = 5000;
client.onError([](const pdg::NetError& error) {
    // Report error.code and error.message to the application.
}).connect(endpoint, [&](pdg::NetConnectionPtr connected) {
    connection = connected;
    connection->onMessage([](pdg::NetConnection& peer,
                             const pdg::NetMessage& message,
                             pdg::NetDelivery delivery) {
        // Retain a copy of message if processing it after this callback.
    });
    connection->send(std::string("hello"));
});
// Call runtime.poll() from the application's update loop.
\endcode

The server listen callback runs after authentication and protocol negotiation.
Return true to accept the connection or false to close it. onReady receives the
actual bound listener ports, including ephemeral port requests (port zero).
Requested listeners start atomically unless allowPartialListen is true.
Startup errors and connection errors are delivered through onError. Invalid
arguments and incorrect thread/state usage throw standard C++ exceptions;
synchronous transport/frame-limit failures can throw pdg::NetError.
Callbacks may send or close connections and change handlers. Callback exceptions
propagate out of poll(); applications should handle them at the poll boundary.

\section native_network_messages Messages and serialization

send() accepts UTF-8 strings, byte spans, pdg::NetMessage, or pdg::ISerializable.
sendJson() accepts validated JSON text. NetMessage::memBlock() carries owned
bytes with the JavaScript MemBlock wire tag. JSON stays text in C++; parse it
with the application's JSON library. Strings reject embedded NUL and invalid
UTF-8. Byte payloads preserve embedded zero bytes.

Both send and sendDgram serialize ISerializable immediately, including its
class tag. Register the receiving C++ factory with
`pdg::IDeserializer::registerClass<MyMessage>()`; register the equivalent
JavaScript class with `pdg.registerSerializableClass(MyMessage)`.
The class tag and field layout must match. Incoming NetMessage::object() owns
the reconstructed object through a shared pointer, releasing PDG's reference
when the last message copy disappears. Unknown classes or malformed data close
the connection with ERR_REMOTE_BAD_DATA.

Outgoing buffers are copied before send returns. Incoming messages own their
text, bytes, and objects; callbacks receive a const reference valid for the
callback duration. Copying NetMessage retains the data for later use.

sendDgram() uses negotiated UDP/WebTransport datagrams when available and within
the carrier size limit. Otherwise it uses the reliable stream, including over
WebSocket, for all supported message types. NetDelivery reports the actual
carrier: Reliable or Unreliable. hasDgram() becomes true only after negotiation.
An unavailable UDP port leaves native TCP available for reliable fallback.
Native UDP supports up to 1492 serialized bytes; WebTransport uses its advertised
maximum (currently 1100). Reliable payloads must fit maxFrameSize.

\section native_network_options Transports and limits

| Option | Default / behavior |
|--------|--------------------|
| NetServerOptions::native | true; native TCP plus UDP unless noDatagram is true |
| serverAddr / serverPort | 0.0.0.0 / 5000; zero requests an ephemeral port |
| fixedPort | false; native bind tries up to 100 consecutive TCP ports |
| webSocket.enabled / webTransport.enabled | false; enable listeners independently |
| Web listener port / path / secure | 5443 / /pdg / true; secure=false applies to WebSocket |
| Web listener allowedOrigins | empty; supply exact origins or `*` for development; origin-less clients need `""` or `*` |
| Web listener TLS | certFile and keyFile required for secure listeners |
| NetConnectOptions::transportPolicy | Auto; explicit WebTransport URL preferred, then WebSocket, otherwise native |
| NativeOnly / WebSocketOnly / WebTransportRequired | Require the selected carrier |
| timeout / webTransportTimeout | 5000 / 1500 milliseconds, total establishment / automatic WT attempt |
| capabilityCacheTimeout | 30000 milliseconds; zero disables the negative capability cache |
| tls.caFile | Optional private CA PEM file; otherwise use system trust |
| tls.serverCertificateHashes | Optional WebTransport SHA-256 DER digests, lowercase hex |
| reservationRequired | false; expectClient keys are checked before application callbacks |
| expectClient ttl / singleUse | -1 seconds (no expiry) / false; IP `*` matches any client |
| maxFrameSize / maxPendingBytes | 1 MiB / 4 MiB; frame range 1 byte–64 MiB, pending range 1 KiB–64 MiB |
| handshakeTimeout | 5000 milliseconds; zero disables the PDG handshake deadline |
| maxConnections / maxConnectionsPerIP / maxConnectionsPerOrigin | 1024 / 32 / 1024 |
| maxConnectionsPerMinute / maxIncompleteHandshakes | 120 per IP / 128 |
| idleTimeout | 30000 milliseconds for the WebTransport backend |

Automatic fallback is sequential and uses secure WebSocket when WebTransport
is unavailable, unsupported, or times out before establishment. Certificate,
authentication, and PDG protocol failures do not trigger fallback. A ws:// URL
explicitly requests plaintext WebSocket. Native TCP/UDP is plaintext; use WSS or
WebTransport for authenticated encryption. C++ servers are supported on desktop;
iOS provides clients only. This facade cannot be built for WASM.

\section native_network_build SDK and verification

The root CMake option provides the imported pdg-net target, linked to pdg-lib and
the bundled transport archive, and the pdg-native-network-tests executable.
For an iOS native SDK, configure `src/sys/net` separately with
`-DCMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME=iOS`, the required SDK/architecture, and a deployment target.
Link libpdg_net.a, webtransport/libpdg_webtransport_all.a, the matching native
PDG core, Security, and CoreFoundation. All dependencies are static C/C++.
FETCHCONTENT_SOURCE_DIR_PICOQUIC, PICOTLS, MBEDTLS, and CJSON overrides support
using an existing source cache without downloading dependencies.

Run `pdg-native-network-tests --selftest` or CTest's native-network test.
Run `tools/node test/lib/native_network.integration.js <test-executable> <pdg>`
for C++ ↔ JavaScript TCP, WS, WSS, and WebTransport protocol tests.
*/
